questions.answer:
Short answer: Create with insertOne/insertMany, read with find/findOne, update with updateOne/updateMany/replaceOne, and delete with deleteOne/deleteMany. Writes return a result object with counts, reads return documents.

How it works: Return values in the Node.js driver:
insertOne: { acknowledged, insertedId }
insertMany: { insertedCount, insertedIds }
findOne: one document or null
find: a cursor (not an array)
updateOne/updateMany: { matchedCount, modifiedCount, upsertedId }
deleteOne/deleteMany: { deletedCount }

Example: After a "delete account" request you check deletedCount. If it is 0, the user id did not exist, so you return 404 instead of "deleted".

Common mistake: Expecting findOne to throw when nothing matches. It returns null, so code like user.name crashes with "Cannot read properties of null".

Interview tip: Mention that you always check the counts (matchedCount, deletedCount) to give correct API responses, instead of assuming the write did something.

